      ],
      "margin_number": null,
      "speaker": null,
      "text": "(a) Do not use φημί if you are translating English ‘say that…’ into Greek. Use\nλέγω ὅτι for the moment. See further Reference Grammar 397.",
      "lang": "mixed",
      "para_no": null,
      "marker": null,
      "table": null,
      "exercise": null,
      "caption": null,
      "uncertain": []
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "order": 5,
      "bbox": [
        453,
        187,
        489,
        907
      ],
      "margin_number": null,
      "speaker": null,
      "text": "(b) You will find φάσκων (which is ‘borrowed’ from the verb φάσκω) used far\nmore commonly than φάς as the present participle of φημί.",
      "lang": "mixed",
      "para_no": null,
      "marker": null,
      "table": null,
      "exercise": null,
      "caption": null,
      "uncertain": []
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "order": 6,
      "bbox": [
        492,
        187,
        528,
        907
      ],
      "margin_number": null,
      "speaker": null,
      "text": "(c) In passages containing direct speech, you will often find ἦν δ’ ἐγώ, ‘said I’,\nand ἦ δ’ ὅς ‘said he’ used as alternatives to φημί and φησί.",
      "lang": "mixed",
      "para_no": null,
