      "margin_number": 168,
      "speaker": null,
      "text": "168b. Usually, in sentences with two nouns either side of the verb ‘to be’, the\ncomplement lacks a definite article (45–7). But not always. In answer to\nthe question ‘Who are the learners, the clever or the stupid?’, Greek will\nsay οἱ σοφοί εἰσιν οἱ μανθάνοντες ‘The learners are the (= those who are)\nclever’, i.e. οἱ creates the group. What would it mean if it lacked οἱ? (As\nusual, the complement comes first.)",
      "lang": "mixed",
      "para_no": null,
      "marker": null,
      "table": null,
